starbucks isn't doing that great in fact  it's down 40 year-to-date and it's  currently the 474th worst performing  stock in the s p 500. this correlates to  a total market cap loss of 66.4  billion dollars given how the overall  market is doing though i bet a lot of  you guys aren't even surprised the  nasdaq and russell 2000 are officially  in bear markets and the s p 500 is  teetering between correction and bear  market territory but while it's easy to  just write off starbucks sell-off as a  result of a weak market there's actually  a lot of background factors that are  prompting this sell-off and if you don't  believe me just take a look at this  last month the retired father of  starbucks howard schultz returned  starbucks to change the direction of the  company this isn't the first time howard  is leaving retirement to run starbucks  either  back in 2008 during the height of the  financial recession howard returned  starbucks as ceo and he led the company  up until 2017. during this time period  starbucks stock grew an impressive at  17x from the 2008 bottom but the company  hasn't done all that much since howard  left in fact the stock is almost back to  the same price that you left it at this  has caused many investors to question if  anybody can run starbucks other than  howard but before we get into that we  have to look into whether howard can  even save starbucks again in the first  place so what even happened to starbucks  one of the top factors driving down  starbucks is worldwide shortages in  almost everything starting with labor if  you haven't heard already a significant  number of employees especially in the us  have been quitting their jobs over the  past few years  in fact in 2021 47 million americans  voluntarily quit their jobs and if you  didn't notice that's literally  one-fourth of the entire u.s labor force  given that the unemployment rate isn't  skyrocketing though it's clear that  these guys aren't just leaving the  corporate world altogether  rather they're switching over to  companies and jobs where they feel more  respected and appreciated naturally you  would think that compensation plays a  big factor in this decision as well but  this is actually not the case  in fact dissatisfaction with one's  salary does not even rank in the top 10  reasons that people are leaving their  jobs salary dissatisfaction actually  comes in at rank 16. some of the reasons  that people are really switching jobs is  a toxic corporate culture and  underappreciated work and there's no  question that these issues are  especially prevalent within service  oriented industries like starbucks this  trend has made it extremely difficult  for starbucks to fill positions and keep  them filled and it's not just a case of  oh we're missing a cashier or a barista  today no starbucks is literally missing  half of their workforce you see when the  pandemic hit starbucks laid off the vast  majority of their employees their  employee account fell from 349 000  employees to 138 000 employees meanwhile  the number of stores starbucks was  operating never went down it actually  kept going up  we don't yet know how many of these  positions starbucks has filled since  last september but i think it's pretty  safe to assume that it's not the full  211 000 positions this means that the  remaining employees are having to do  even more work which is just further  motivating them to switch jobs the  staffing shortage has actually gotten so  bad that some starbucks locations have  started reducing store hours and as a  business that's usually the last thing  you want to do especially if there's  sufficient demand aside from facing a  severe staffing shortage starbucks is  also having to deal with the supply  shortage if you go to your local  starbucks it's more than likely that at  least a few of the menu items are  unavailable due to a lack of ingredients  some of the hardest hit ingredients  include oat milk flavored syrups and tea  bags but the shortage also extends to  some of starbucks most popular offerings  as well  some locations don't even have  frappuccino mix or breakfast sandwiches  and just a few months ago the entire  region of upstate new york ran out of  mocha so not only are starbucks  employees having to do more work but  they're also having to constantly deal  with customers that are upset that they  can't even get what they came for and  the longer the shortages keep up it's  just gonna become harder and harder for  starbucks to recover  the supply shortage isn't the only  reason that customers are upset either  another issue that is driving up  customer dissatisfaction is rising  prices now it's no secret that starbucks  is already a premium brand with  expensive offerings many people have  looked past these prices for years due  to convenience and taste however as  starbucks continues to raise prices it's  becoming harder and harder for customers  to justify going to starbucks  the worst part is that this is not  something that starbucks even has  control over prices across the board are  simply skyrocketing and they have to  raise prices to keep up for example the  price of coffee has doubled since 2019  from 100 a pound to over 200 per pound  similarly i'm sure that all of the  equipment that starbucks locations along  with their cups spoons and straws have  also risen in price due to  out-of-control inflation and on top of  all of this starbucks is having to raise  wages to retain the employees that they  do have last october they committed to  increasing minimum wage across the  country to 15 per hour now i'm not  trying to argue whether such wage hikes  are justified or not but one thing  that's certain is that someone has to  pay for these higher wages and more  times than not that someone is the  customer meanwhile from the customer's  perspective it's not just starbucks  where they're having to spend more money  over the past year the cost of living  has skyrocketed everything from gas and  energy bills to grocery and housing  costs have gone to the moon while wages  haven't and there's only so many price  increases that consumers can afford  before they start to decrease their  consumption necessities like gas and the  groceries aren't something you can avoid  but something you can avoid is  overpriced starbucks coffee and as  inflation and continues to remain high  we'll likely just see more and more  people cut back on such purchases  something else that's also leading to  customer and employee dissatisfaction in  starbuck's mobile slash online order  system embracing a mobile order system  was a no-brainer during the pandemic but  long term it seems to have caused more  headaches than solutions here's the  thing when people come to the store and  order in person there's a natural limit  to how long the line will get i mean if  there's already 20 people in line the  people who come after are more likely to  just skip starbucks for the day  but when a significant number of people  are ordering online and there's a long  line at the store fulfilling all these  orders quickly becomes overwhelming  especially during rush hours with a  staffing shortage and when these online  customers show up at the store they're  often upset that their order isn't ready  yet something else to note is that when  people order online they're way more  likely to make more complex orders  because it's easier to click buttons  than to verbally communicate such an  order this has made fulfilling online  orders more time consuming which just  just made it even harder to keep up with  rush hour demand to make things worse  their online system often doesn't  reflect missing ingredients meaning that  customers are often allowed to put in  orders that can't be made this leaves  the employees with two options either  they can inform the customer that their  order can't be completed when they show  up at the store which is probably not  the most fun thing to do or they can  substitute the missing ingredient with  an alternative that is available but  this doesn't necessarily lead to the  happiest of customers either the  straightforward solution to this  conundrum is for starbucks to revamp  their online system and implement two  features  first of all their online system should  limit orders to a manageable volume and  secondly their online system should  accurately represent shortages  if they don't revamp their online system  we'll likely just continue to see more  misunderstandings between customers and  employees causing starbucks to lose  customers over the long term  while all of the factors that we've  discussed so far are definitely weighing  down on starbucks business likely the  biggest reason behind howard's return is  starbucks employees unionizing for  service-based businesses with hundreds  of thousands of minimum wage workers  unions are often their biggest fear  here's the thing while starbucks charges  a ridiculous amount for their beverages  they don't actually take that much of it  to the bottom line historically  starbucks net margin has hovered at just  under 15 percent and while this is a  solid amount for a restaurant business  this doesn't give them much leeway in  terms of raising wages  so starbucks and other low margin  companies like amazon are extremely  anti-union but despite their attempts to  stifle unions we've seen starbucks  employees put up quite a fight over the  past year for example remember how  starbucks committed to increasing their  minimum wage to 15 per hour well this  was a last-ditch effort to try to stifle  union participation before they hiked  wages they were attempting to terminate  every union member they could find they  even sent managers to starbucks  locations to try to dissuade union  participation and it looks like  starbucks is simply doubling down on  crushing unions  at the beginning of may howard announced  a set of races that will only benefit  non-unionized workers and he justified  this decision with likely the most  garbage reason you have ever heard  howard suggested that he couldn't raise  wages for unionized workers because that  would be imposing new wages without the  union's approval starbucks workers  united has fired back by filing charges  against howard for discriminating  against union workers  this is still a very fluid situation so  we'll have to wait and see how this  battle plays out but honestly i think  howard's best choice is to simply give  in to the unions here's the thing howard  can play this game as much as he wants  and he might even win against the unions  but i think howard's victory would  actually be a loss for starbucks over  the years starbucks has maintained a  pretty respectable image when it comes  to employee benefits and treatment but  pushing vigorously against unions can  easily destroy that image and i don't  think that's in starbucks best interest  given that this is the primary reason  that howard has returned though i  suspect that this is going to be a  bloody battle  at the end of the day starbucks is a  mess both internally and externally  they're plagued with staff and supply  shortages while having to deal with  insane inflation and bad software which  is leading to pretty unhappy customers  on top of this starbucks is ferociously  fighting against internal unionization  efforts which have thus far proven quite  successful howard returning to starbucks  simply enunciates how critical the  situation is and unfortunately there is  no easy solution if howard gives into  the union starbucks will likely have to  deal with even higher operating expenses  which will just make it even harder for  them to retain customers on the other  hand if howard doesn't give in starbucks  will not only continue to face a  staffing shortage but they'll probably  tarnish their reputation  and in my opinion their long-term  reputation is worth a lot more than  short-term profits but with that being  said if anyone can get starbucks back on  track it's howard so we'll have to wait  and see what he does do you guys believe  howard can turn starbucks around comment  that down below  also drop a like if you think starbucks  is overrated and of course consider  checking out our international channels  to watch your videos in other languages  and consider subscribing to see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm hari and i'll see you guys on  the next one
 

